# Certificates recording what a gate last validated, written by
# scripts/commit-gate-stamp.sh and checked by the pre-commit hook. They describe
# one clone's state, so they are never committed — but the directory is, so a
# fresh clone has somewhere to put them and this ignore rule is in force from
# the first commit rather than after someone remembers to add it.
#
# The rule is load-bearing, not tidiness. A certificate is a hash of the working
# tree; a *tracked* certificate would be part of the tree it measures, so writing
# one would change the answer and no certificate could ever match twice. The
# script refuses to write a certificate that git does not ignore, so if this rule
# is ever edited away the gate fails loudly instead of silently rejecting every
# commit.
*
!.gitignore
